Durarara!! AU Chapter 2 – Part 1
Rio eventually let go.
She stepped back, wiped at her face once with the sleeve of her shirt, and gave Curtis a small, unsteady nod. No more words. Just that. Then she turned and started walking, shoulders still a little hunched, but her feet moving forward instead of toward another rooftop.
Curtis watched her go until she reached the corner and disappeared from sight.
Only then did he notice the other presence.
A woman in black leather had approached without making a sound. She wore a full-face yellow helmet with small cat-ear accents on top, the visor dark enough that nothing of her face could be seen. The leather suit fit close, practical, built for riding. She stopped a few paces away and simply stood there, the empty space where a head should have been somehow still managing to feel directed at him.
Rio had walked right past her without slowing down.
The woman lifted her phone and typed with practiced speed. she turned the phone to him
“You saved that girl.”
She didn’t move closer. Instead her helmet turned slightly toward the side of the building. The spiderweb cracks Curtis had left in the concrete were still visible, pale and sharp under the streetlights. Her attention shifted downward next, to the fractured pavement where he had landed with Rio in his arms.
Curtis studied her in silence.
The black leather. The yellow helmet. The way she stood like the shadows themselves had decided to take a shape and walk. He took in the lack of any visible breath, the absolute stillness of her posture, the faint residual trace of something dark that had already pulled back into the alley behind her.
He didn’t speak right away. He simply looked at her the way he looked at most new things in this city—calm, measuring, and without the usual human need to fill the quiet.
Curtis kept his eyes on the yellow helmet.
“And?” he said, voice low and even behind the mask. “It seems you were going to do the same.”
The woman in black leather didn’t shift her weight or make any unnecessary movement. She simply lifted the PDA again and typed with quick, precise taps. The artificial voice that followed was the same flat, clear tone as before.
“Yes. I was.”
A short pause, then more typing.
“I saw her on the edge. I was already moving when you reached her first. You were faster.”
The helmet tilted slightly toward the cracked wall, then down to the fractured pavement at Curtis’s feet. Another few taps.
“Those cracks… normal people cannot do that. The wall, the ground. You took the fall and the girl without injury. That is not ordinary strength.”
She lowered the Phone a fraction, the dark visor of the yellow helmet still fixed on him.
“I do not know what you are. But you saved her. That is enough for me not to interfere further tonight.”
Celty remained still, waiting to see whether the tall, masked man would offer anything else—or simply walk away the same way the girl had.
Celty remained still for a few seconds.
Then, unexpectedly, she spoke.
The womens voice that came out from beneath the yellow helmet was quiet,and carried a faint, uncertain edge—like someone testing whether the words would even work.
“…What are you?”
She tilted the helmet slightly, the dark visor still locked on Curtis. The question wasn’t hostile. It sounded closer to genuine confusion mixed with caution.
“I’ve seen a lot of things in this city. People with strength. People who aren’t entirely human. But the way you moved… the cracks in the wall… catching her like that without even bracing…”
A short pause.
“You didn’t hesitate. Most people freeze. Or look away. You just… decided she wasn’t going to hit the ground.”
Celty’s gloved fingers flexed once at her side.
“You’re not from around here. That much is obvious. And you’re hiding your face for a reason. I don’t care about that. But I do care about what walks through Ikebukuro at night when it can do what you just did.”
She fell quiet again, the yellow helmet angled toward him, waiting.
The phone stayed at her side this time. For once, the Headless Rider had chosen to speak with her own.
Curtis answered without raising his voice.
“I hide my face because I feel like it. No one’s after me. And I won’t stand by while someone tries to kill themselves.”
The yellow helmet stayed locked on him.
For several seconds Celty didn’t move at all. Her gloved fingers hovered over the Phone screen, frozen mid-motion.She hadn’t typed a single word of the question that had just been in her mind.
Yet he had answered it.
A sharp, almost startled tension ran through her shoulders. The Headless Rider took half a step back before she caught herself. In all the years she had spent in Ikebukuro, no one had ever responded to something she hadn’t put through the PDA. People couldn’t hear her. That was the entire reason the device existed.
She stared at the masked man in front of her, the dark visor of the yellow helmet giving nothing away while her thoughts raced.
He heard me.
Slowly, carefully, she lifted the Phone and typed with deliberate precision. The artificial voice finally spoke, flatter than usual.
“…How did you know what I was going to ask?”
The question hung in the night air between them. Celty remained completely still, waiting, the cracks in the wall and pavement suddenly feeling a lot more significant than they had a minute ago.
